.\" robodr-seal(8). Source of truth: the USAGE text in
.\" crates/robodr-seal/src/cli.rs, the crate docs and
.\" docs/SECURE-DELIVERY.md (sections 7 and 10). Keep them in sync.
.TH ROBODR\-SEAL 8 "2026-10-30" "RoboDR 0.1.0-alpha.1" "RoboDR Manual"
.SH NAME
robodr\-seal \- seal delivered software to a unit's TPM: keys, signed PCR policies, runtime volume, crypto-erase
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B robodr\-seal keygen depot|policy \-\-out
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-seal policy sign \-\-key
.I FILE
.B \-\-platform
.I P
.B \-\-version
.I N
.B \-\-pcr
.IR I = HEX ...
.RB [ \-\-floor\-nv
.IR 0xNNNNNNNN ]
.B \-\-out
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-seal provision
.I TPM
.RB [ \-\-owner\-auth\-file
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-seal enroll \-\-seal\-dir
.I DIR
.B \-\-unit
.I U
.B \-\-platform
.I P
.B \-\-policy\-authority
.I FILE.pub
.I TPM
.br
.B robodr\-seal enroll\-fallback \-\-seal\-dir
.I DIR
.B \-\-unit
.I U
.B \-\-platform
.I P
.B \-\-key
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-seal policy install|commit \-\-seal\-dir
.I DIR
.RB [ \-\-policy
.IR FILE ]
.I TPM
.br
.B robodr\-seal rewrap \-\-depot\-key
.I FILE
.B \-\-rpk
.I DIR
.B \-\-unit\-record
.I UNIT.json
.br
.B robodr\-seal volume format|open|format\-tang|open\-tang|close
.I ...
.br
.B robodr\-seal erase \-\-seal\-dir
.I DIR
.B \-\-trigger
.I T
.RB [ \-\-device
.IR DEV ]
.RI [ TPM ]
.br
.B robodr\-seal status \-\-seal\-dir
.I DIR
.RI [ TPM ]
.PP
.I TPM
is
.RB [ \-\-tcti
.IR TCTI ]
.RB [ \-\-tools\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-srk
.IR 0x81NNNNNN ]
.RB [ \-\-epoch\-nv
.IR 0x01NNNNNN ]
.RB [ \-\-floor\-nv
.IR 0x01NNNNNN ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout\-ms
.IR MS ]
(defaults
.IR device:/dev/tpmrm0 ,
.IR /usr/bin ,
0x81000001, 0x01500040, 0x01500041, 10000).
.SH DESCRIPTION
Captured-unit software protection of RoboDR's secure delivery (Sprint
13c). A sealed package (RPK) is encrypted with a fresh content key
(XChaCha20-Poly1305 per chunk); the release wraps that key to the site
depots' X25519 keys, a depot re-wraps it to each unit's
.B UWK
(unit wrapping key), and the unit unwraps it only into its encrypted
runtime volume.
.PP
On a unit with a TPM 2.0 the UWK is a P-256 key created inside the TPM
.RB ( enroll ),
usable only under a policy: a PCR policy signed by the
.B policy authority
for the unit's platform, whose version the TPM's
.B policy floor
(an NV bit field) has not revoked, and while the TPM's
.B erase epoch
(an NV counter) is unchanged. The TPM is driven with
.BR tpm2\-tools ;
the shared secret is computed in an encrypted, salted policy session and
never written to a file. A unit without a TPM keeps an X25519 UWK inside
a runtime volume that opens only through the site's Tang server.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B keygen depot
An X25519 depot wrapping key: secret
.I FILE
(0600) and
.IR FILE .pub
(what
.B robodr\-release build rpk \-\-seal\-to
takes).
.TP
.B keygen policy
The P-256 policy authority key (offline, with the delivery roots) and its
public file (pinned into every UWK at enrolment).
.TP
.B policy sign
Sign the approved policy for SHA-256 PCR values
.RB ( \-\-pcr " index=value, 1..24) and version " N " (0..63)."
.TP
.B provision
Define the erase-epoch counter, the policy-floor bit field and the
persistent SRK (idempotent; an index with other attributes is refused,
never redefined). Set an owner authorization first and pass it with
.B \-\-owner\-auth\-file
(read by the tools on stdin).
.TP
.B enroll
Create the UWK in the TPM and print the enrolment record (public) for
the depot.
.TP
.B enroll\-fallback
Create an X25519 UWK in
.I FILE
(inside the open runtime volume) for a unit without a TPM.
.TP
.B policy install
Install a signed policy (checked against the enrolled authority,
platform and floor; a revoked version is refused).
.TP
.B policy commit
Unseal a probe key under the installed policy, then revoke every lower
version in the TPM. Run it only after the first boot that unseals with a
new policy.
.TP
.B rewrap
The depot's step: print the deployment
.B wrapped_keys
entry of RPK
.I DIR
for the unit of
.IR UNIT.json .
.TP
.B volume format
Create the LUKS2 runtime volume on
.B \-\-device
with a random key stored only wrapped to the UWK;
.B volume open
opens it as
.B \-\-name
(device-mapper).
.TP
.B volume format\-tang
Fallback: LUKS2 bound only to the Tang server
.RB ( \-\-tang\-url ,
.BR \-\-tang\-thp );
.B volume open\-tang
opens it while the server is reachable.
.TP
.B erase
Crypto-erase: increment the erase epoch (every key sealed to the unit is
dead), delete the UWK blobs; fallback: destroy the UWK file. With
.B \-\-device
also wipe every LUKS2 keyslot. Final.
.B robodr\-deliverd erase
does the same with journal entries and findings.
.TP
.B status
Mode, erase state, enrolled and current epoch, policy version, floor bits.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/robodr\-seal/
The seal directory (0700): unit.json (enrolment record), uwk.pub and
uwk.priv (the UWK's TPM blobs, useless without that TPM), policy.json,
volume.wrap, erased.json.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 ok, 1 refused or error, 2 usage.
.SH LIMITS
Tested against swtpm only, not on a hardware TPM; the dm-crypt open path
is tested only where device-mapper is available. The policy authority is
one key (the TPM cannot enforce the roots' quorum). The UWK is not yet
certified by the TPM's endorsement key (Sprint 14a): a depot must get
enrolment records over a trusted channel. Erase is final and not yet
triggered automatically (Sprint 14b).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR robodr\-deliverd (8),
